WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.820
In 1692, Richard
Coman told a Salem

00:00:02.940 --> 00:00:04.340
court about a night no one else

00:00:04.380 --> 00:00:06.520
could see. He said the house was

00:00:06.580 --> 00:00:09.000
locked. A light was burning.

00:00:09.060 --> 00:00:10.320
His wife lay beside him.

00:00:11.051 --> 00:00:12.740
Then Bridget Bishop
entered the room.

00:00:13.340 --> 00:00:15.140
Coman said she lay
across his chest

00:00:15.380 --> 00:00:17.350
and pressed him down
so completely that

00:00:17.360 --> 00:00:19.740
he could neither speak
nor move—not even

00:00:19.780 --> 00:00:20.920
enough to wake his wife.

00:00:21.840 --> 00:00:23.800
Years after the
night itself, he put

00:00:23.820 --> 00:00:25.060
that memory under oath.

00:00:25.900 --> 00:00:28.360
Eight days after his
testimony, Bridget Bishop

00:00:28.400 --> 00:00:31.190
was hanged. Coman's
statement did not create

00:00:31.191 --> 00:00:32.200
the case against her.

00:00:32.759 --> 00:00:35.250
It entered a much larger
prosecution already

00:00:35.280 --> 00:00:37.280
crowded with accusations.

00:00:37.320 --> 00:00:40.060
But it shows something
extraordinary: a private

00:00:40.110 --> 00:00:42.840
experience had crossed
into a courtroom, attached

00:00:42.900 --> 00:00:44.900
itself to a living
woman, and become

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:46.340
part of public reality.

00:00:46.960 --> 00:00:48.730
The record cannot
tell us what state

00:00:48.800 --> 00:00:50.100
produced Coman's night.

00:00:50.820 --> 00:00:52.340
It tells us what Salem did with

00:00:52.400 --> 00:00:54.550
it. So how does an experience no

00:00:54.640 --> 00:00:57.420
one else can witness
become evidence—and can

00:00:57.480 --> 00:01:00.000
the explanation a culture
gives that experience

00:01:00.010 --> 00:01:01.180
change the next night?

00:01:02.140 --> 00:01:03.820
Look closely at Coman's sequence.

00:01:04.480 --> 00:01:06.060
He is awake in a familiar room.

00:01:06.660 --> 00:01:07.560
The door is locked.

00:01:08.100 --> 00:01:09.260
A figure appears.

00:01:09.320 --> 00:01:10.300
His voice fails.

00:01:10.780 --> 00:01:12.100
His body will not answer.

00:01:12.780 --> 00:01:14.580
Pressure settles across his chest.

00:01:15.420 --> 00:01:17.200
Today, the pattern
is recognizable.

00:01:17.560 --> 00:01:19.300
But Salem had no language of REM

00:01:19.380 --> 00:01:20.900
sleep or muscle atonia.

00:01:21.540 --> 00:01:24.000
It had another language
ready: witchcraft.

00:01:24.440 --> 00:01:27.040
And Salem did not
invent the pattern.

00:01:27.080 --> 00:01:30.240
In 1781, Henry Fuseli
painted The Nightmare.

00:01:31.180 --> 00:01:32.900
A woman lies thrown back across a

00:01:32.940 --> 00:01:35.040
bed. A squat figure sits on her

00:01:35.080 --> 00:01:37.860
chest. From behind
a curtain, a horse

00:01:37.960 --> 00:01:40.040
stares into the room
with white, impossible

00:01:40.200 --> 00:01:43.120
eyes. The painting
became famous not because

00:01:43.160 --> 00:01:45.280
it explained the
night, but because it

00:01:45.340 --> 00:01:46.420
gave the night a body.

00:01:47.280 --> 00:01:49.420
Across Europe, the
nightmare was once more

00:01:49.460 --> 00:01:50.440
than a bad dream.

00:01:51.160 --> 00:01:54.030
The old word carried
weight: the mare,

00:01:54.280 --> 00:01:56.460
or mara, a visitor that rides or

00:01:56.480 --> 00:01:58.160
presses the sleeper.

00:01:58.200 --> 00:02:00.280
Christian Europe
imagined the incubus.

00:02:01.060 --> 00:02:04.030
In Japan, people spoke
of kanashibari—being

00:02:04.060 --> 00:02:05.240
bound as if by metal.

00:02:05.760 --> 00:02:08.020
In Newfoundland, the
visitor became the Old

00:02:08.120 --> 00:02:11.440
Hag. Other traditions
gave pressure, paralysis

00:02:11.450 --> 00:02:13.870
and nocturnal presence
their own agents and

00:02:13.940 --> 00:02:16.620
names. These figures
do not form one

00:02:16.740 --> 00:02:18.500
clean family tree.

00:02:18.560 --> 00:02:20.410
No single demon can
be followed from

00:02:20.500 --> 00:02:21.820
one language to the next.

00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:24.140
What repeats is more basic.

00:02:24.750 --> 00:02:25.650
The room is known.

00:02:25.910 --> 00:02:26.880
The body is trapped.

00:02:27.620 --> 00:02:30.280
Something approaches.
Pressure gains intention.

00:02:30.940 --> 00:02:33.220
Perhaps the story
begins because the body

00:02:33.300 --> 00:02:35.960
produces an experience
that demands a cause.

00:02:36.860 --> 00:02:38.510
But once a culture
has supplied that

00:02:38.640 --> 00:02:40.780
cause, the next
person does not enter

00:02:40.840 --> 00:02:42.020
the night empty-handed.

00:02:42.740 --> 00:02:45.260
In early modern Europe,
a nocturnal attack

00:02:45.280 --> 00:02:47.420
belonged to a world
of sin, spirits,

00:02:47.720 --> 00:02:49.340
curses and divine protection.

00:02:49.980 --> 00:02:51.700
If the cause was an acting being,

00:02:51.950 --> 00:02:53.480
the response had to be an action:

00:02:54.080 --> 00:02:57.240
prayer, a cross, iron,
water, a household

00:02:57.420 --> 00:02:58.840
object placed near the bed.

00:02:59.500 --> 00:03:01.460
From the outside,
these acts can look

00:03:01.500 --> 00:03:04.540
irrational. From inside
that world, they are

00:03:04.580 --> 00:03:07.560
practical. If the ritual
brings relief, the

00:03:07.620 --> 00:03:09.300
explanation feels confirmed.

00:03:09.980 --> 00:03:11.820
That is how the
bedroom opens outward.

00:03:12.480 --> 00:03:14.630
An episode can support
a family story.

00:03:15.120 --> 00:03:16.080
A religious order.

00:03:16.700 --> 00:03:19.660
An accusation. In
Salem, it could enter

00:03:19.680 --> 00:03:20.900
the machinery of a court.

00:03:21.500 --> 00:03:23.670
The simplest modern
answer would be that

00:03:23.700 --> 00:03:25.790
people see the beings
their culture taught

00:03:25.800 --> 00:03:27.060
them to see.

00:03:27.100 --> 00:03:29.010
Then David Hufford
creates a problem for

00:03:29.060 --> 00:03:32.060
that answer. Hufford
studied the Old Hag

00:03:32.140 --> 00:03:33.360
tradition in Newfoundland.

00:03:33.860 --> 00:03:35.870
He also knew the
experience personally.

00:03:36.530 --> 00:03:38.680
What mattered most
to his work, however,

00:03:38.700 --> 00:03:40.740
were people who described
the familiar pattern

00:03:40.760 --> 00:03:42.490
before they knew
the local story that

00:03:42.520 --> 00:03:44.250
named it. Paralysis.

00:03:44.580 --> 00:03:46.480
Pressure. Presence first.

00:03:47.109 --> 00:03:49.020
The Old Hag later.

00:03:49.060 --> 00:03:50.660
Culture did not have to invent the

00:03:50.680 --> 00:03:53.660
entire event. A recurring
human state could

00:03:53.700 --> 00:03:56.180
come first, and communities
could build different

00:03:56.220 --> 00:03:58.300
explanations around it.

00:03:58.440 --> 00:04:00.440
That helps explain
why the same bodily

00:04:00.450 --> 00:04:02.700
structure can appear
in traditions with no

00:04:02.820 --> 00:04:03.960
simple line of descent.

00:04:04.600 --> 00:04:06.060
But it opens a harder question.

00:04:06.900 --> 00:04:08.340
If culture does not have to create

00:04:08.380 --> 00:04:10.280
the first episode, what can it do

00:04:10.360 --> 00:04:11.260
to the second?

00:04:11.640 --> 00:04:13.680
Imagine two people
waking into the same

00:04:13.720 --> 00:04:16.579
paralysis. One knows
the state will pass.

00:04:16.940 --> 00:04:18.950
The other has heard
since childhood that

00:04:19.000 --> 00:04:20.570
this is the moment a real being

00:04:20.600 --> 00:04:22.980
attacks—and that
people can die in its

00:04:23.020 --> 00:04:25.380
grasp. The body may begin in the

00:04:25.460 --> 00:04:27.780
same place. Does the night end in

00:04:27.820 --> 00:04:28.720
the same place?

00:04:29.340 --> 00:04:31.580
A research team
led by Baland Jalal

00:04:31.620 --> 00:04:34.400
and Devon Hinton compared
reports from Egypt

00:04:34.420 --> 00:04:37.920
and Denmark. Sleep
paralysis appeared in both

00:04:37.960 --> 00:04:40.160
settings, but its
meaning did not arrive

00:04:40.180 --> 00:04:41.680
in the same form.

00:04:41.720 --> 00:04:45.540
In the Egyptian sample,
supernatural explanations—especially

00:04:45.620 --> 00:04:47.220
jinn—were far more common.

00:04:47.900 --> 00:04:50.660
In the Danish sample,
physiological explanations

00:04:50.680 --> 00:04:52.020
were more common.

00:04:52.060 --> 00:04:54.440
These were specific
samples, not portraits

00:04:54.480 --> 00:04:55.740
of two entire countries.

00:04:56.400 --> 00:04:58.120
Yet the difference
inside the data was

00:04:58.200 --> 00:05:01.140
striking. Egyptian
participants in the studies

00:05:01.440 --> 00:05:04.930
reported more lifetime
episodes, longer immobility

00:05:04.931 --> 00:05:06.600
and greater fear
of dying during an

00:05:06.700 --> 00:05:09.500
attack. Within the
Egyptian group, believing

00:05:09.540 --> 00:05:11.930
that a supernatural force
caused the paralysis

00:05:11.960 --> 00:05:14.340
was associated with
greater fear and longer

00:05:14.380 --> 00:05:17.880
immobility. Association
is not a one-way spell.

00:05:18.800 --> 00:05:20.480
The studies do not
show that belief

00:05:20.540 --> 00:05:22.280
simply manufactures paralysis.

00:05:22.950 --> 00:05:25.840
They show something more
intimate: interpretation

00:05:25.900 --> 00:05:28.280
and bodily distress
can become entangled.

00:05:28.980 --> 00:05:30.260
Fear can fracture sleep.

00:05:30.860 --> 00:05:33.260
Fractured sleep can make
another episode more

00:05:33.300 --> 00:05:36.640
likely. Another episode
can feel like confirmation.

00:05:37.290 --> 00:05:39.460
The expectation waiting
for the next night

00:05:39.500 --> 00:05:42.040
grows heavier. The
story has entered the

00:05:42.080 --> 00:05:44.140
loop. And that may be the most

00:05:44.260 --> 00:05:46.940
unsettling possibility
in this entire history.

00:05:47.640 --> 00:05:49.120
A being does not have to stand

00:05:49.140 --> 00:05:50.660
physically in the
room for belief in

00:05:50.720 --> 00:05:52.840
it to produce
physical consequences.

00:05:53.490 --> 00:05:54.940
So return to Richard Coman.

00:05:55.280 --> 00:05:57.580
His body supplied
wakefulness, immobility,

00:05:57.820 --> 00:05:58.880
pressure and presence.

00:05:59.800 --> 00:06:02.000
Salem supplied a name, a cause and

00:06:02.020 --> 00:06:03.240
a court willing to hear it.

00:06:03.930 --> 00:06:06.280
The experience moved
outward into testimony.

00:06:06.670 --> 00:06:08.770
The testimony returned
to the community as

00:06:08.800 --> 00:06:10.750
truth. What comes first?

00:06:11.600 --> 00:06:13.700
For one person,
perhaps the experience.

00:06:14.420 --> 00:06:16.860
For a culture,
experience and story may

00:06:16.980 --> 00:06:18.300
already be braided together.

00:06:19.060 --> 00:06:20.800
Every new night
meets images that are

00:06:20.840 --> 00:06:23.760
waiting. Every new
account changes what the

00:06:23.800 --> 00:06:25.080
next person expects.

00:06:25.740 --> 00:06:27.160
That is why the question is not

00:06:27.220 --> 00:06:28.920
simply whether the
visitor was real.

00:06:29.651 --> 00:06:31.420
The deeper question
is what kind of

00:06:31.460 --> 00:06:34.440
reality a visitor can
acquire—inside a body,

00:06:34.820 --> 00:06:37.240
inside a memory,
and finally inside a

00:06:37.300 --> 00:06:38.480
room full of other people.

00:06:39.280 --> 00:06:41.160
Which carries more
power into the next

00:06:41.220 --> 00:06:44.120
night: the EXPERIENCE,
or the STORY?

00:06:45.080 --> 00:06:46.270
Leave one word below.

00:06:46.840 --> 00:06:48.490
Because the body
may open the door.

00:06:49.040 --> 00:06:50.760
But once a culture
gives the darkness

00:06:50.800 --> 00:06:52.590
a face, that face can travel.
